<FoundationLayout
  numeral="VII"
  eyebrow="Foundation VII"
  title="The Authority He Was Given"
  tagline="Why exousia — power handed down from the Father — names no second God."
  description="Why exousia — power handed down from the Father — names no second God."
>

  <h2>The Claim This Piece Answers</h2>

  <p>
    Many of the verses used to prove the Trinity show Yahushua doing something with power. He forgives sins. He commands unclean spirits. He teaches with a voice no scribe ever had. He will judge the world. He says He has power to lay down His life and take it again. The argument that follows is always the same: only God has power like this, so the one who has it must be God.
  </p>

  <p>
    The argument rests on one assumption — that the power is His by nature, owned and not received. Scripture has a word for the kind of power these verses describe, and that word answers the assumption every time it appears.
  </p>

  <h2>The Word Itself</h2>

  <p>
    The Greek of the Apostolic writings uses two main words that the King James translates “power.” They do not mean the same thing.
  </p>

  <div class="ref-word">
    <span class="ref-script" lang="grc">ἐξουσία</span>
    <span class="ref-translit">exousia (G1849)</span>
    <span class="ref-gloss">Authority, right, permission. Strong’s: “privilege … competency, freedom … delegated influence.” Translated power, authority, right, jurisdiction.</span>
  </div>

  <div class="ref-word">
    <span class="ref-script" lang="grc">δύναμις</span>
    <span class="ref-translit">dunamis (G1411)</span>
    <span class="ref-gloss">Force, might, ability — strength itself. Translated power, mighty work, strength, miracle.</span>
  </div>

  <p>
    Exousia is built from the verb <strong>exesti</strong> (G1832), “it is lawful, it is permitted.” At its root, exousia is permission — the right to act that someone with standing has granted. Dunamis is the strength to do a thing. Exousia is the right to do it.
  </p>

  <p>
    A policeman shows the difference plainly. His badge is exousia; his arm is dunamis. The badge was issued to him. Nobody thinks the badge makes the policeman the government that issued it.
  </p>

  <h2>Authority Always Has a Source</h2>

  <p>
    Every time Scripture traces exousia back to where it came from, it finds a giver above the one who holds it:
  </p>

  <p>
    • Rulers: “there is no power but of God: the powers that be are ordained of God” <em>Romans 13:1</em>.<br />
    • Pilate claimed power to crucify and to release, and was told he could have none “except it were given thee from above” <em>John 19:10-11</em>.<br />
    • The master leaving on a journey “gave authority to his servants, and to every man his work” <em>Mark 13:34</em>.<br />
    • Paul spoke of “our authority, which the Lord hath given us” <em>2 Corinthians 10:8</em>.<br />
    • Even the beast holds nothing of his own: “power was given unto him to continue forty and two months” <em>Revelation 13:5</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    From the governor in Jerusalem to the enemy in Revelation, exousia is given. It is never shown as something a being simply has by nature. Only the One at the top of the chain gives it without first receiving it, and Scripture names Him: “there is no power but of God” <em>Romans 13:1</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>The Centurion Understood It</h2>

  <p>
    A Roman centurion asked Yahushua to heal his servant with a word. He explained why he believed it would work: “I am a man under authority, having soldiers under me: and I say to this man, Go, and he goeth” <em>Matthew 8:9</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The centurion’s reasoning was simple. He could command soldiers because he stood under Rome. His orders carried weight because they were backed by an authority above him. He saw the same pattern in Yahushua: a man under authority, whose word carried the weight of the One above Him. Yahushua did not correct him. He “marvelled” and said He had not found such belief in all Israel <em>Matthew 8:10</em>. The centurion was praised for seeing the Son exactly as the Son described Himself — under authority, and therefore able to command.
  </p>

  <h2>Every Time the Son’s Authority Is Traced, It Is Given</h2>

  <p>
    The Son’s authority is described many times. Each time its source is named, the source is the Father:
  </p>

  <p>
    • “All power [exousia] is given unto me in heaven and in earth” <em>Matthew 28:18</em>.<br />
    • The Father “hath given him authority [exousia] to execute judgment also, because he is the Son of man” <em>John 5:27</em>.<br />
    • “As thou hast given him power [exousia] over all flesh” <em>John 17:2</em> — the Son speaking to the Father.<br />
    • “This commandment have I received of my Father” <em>John 10:18</em> — the authority over His own life.<br />
    • “All things are delivered unto me of my Father” <em>Matthew 11:27</em>; “the Father … hath given all things into his hand” <em>John 3:35</em>.<br />
    • Daniel saw one like the Son of man come before the Ancient of days, “and there was given him dominion” <em>Daniel 7:14</em>. The Greek Old Testament renders that dominion with exousia.<br />
    • Risen and glorified, He still says it: “even as I received of my Father” <em>Revelation 2:27</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Not one verse says the Son holds exousia by nature, apart from the Father’s giving. The phrase “given unto me” is the Son’s own description of His authority, and He uses it after the resurrection as much as before it.
  </p>

  <h2>Authority to Forgive</h2>

  <p>
    When Yahushua forgave the sins of a paralyzed man, the scribes said only God can forgive sins. Yahushua answered that “the Son of man hath power [exousia] on earth to forgive sins” <em>Mark 2:10</em> — and He used the title Son of man, the title of a man, to say it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The crowd that watched it happen drew the right conclusion on the spot. “They marvelled, and glorified God, which had given such power unto men” <em>Matthew 9:8</em>. The eyewitnesses did not conclude that God had come down as a second God. They glorified the God who had given exousia — to men.
  </p>

  <p>
    After the resurrection Yahushua handed the same work to His disciples: “Whose soever sins ye remit, they are remitted unto them” <em>John 20:23</em>. If forgiving sins proves deity, the apostles are God. The text proves only that Yahuah forgives, and that He can give the word of forgiveness to whomever He sends.
  </p>

  <h2>Authority to Judge</h2>

  <p>
    The Son will judge the world. The verses that say so also say how He came to hold that office: “The Father judgeth no man, but hath committed all judgment unto the Son” <em>John 5:22</em>, and has given Him authority to judge “because he is the Son of man” <em>John 5:27</em>. Paul told the Athenians that God “will judge the world in righteousness by that man whom he hath ordained” <em>Acts 17:31</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Judgment committed, authority given, a man ordained — three different ways of saying the same thing. The judgment seat is the Father’s, and the Son sits on it by appointment. Believers share in it too: “the saints shall judge the world … we shall judge angels” <em>1 Corinthians 6:2-3</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>Authority to Teach and Command</h2>

  <p>
    At the end of the Sermon on the Mount the people were astonished, “for he taught them as one having authority [exousia], and not as the scribes” <em>Matthew 7:29</em>. In Capernaum they said, “with authority and power he commandeth the unclean spirits” <em>Luke 4:36</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Yahushua told them where the authority of His teaching came from: “I have not spoken of myself; but the Father which sent me, he gave me a commandment, what I should say” <em>John 12:49</em>. His “But I say unto you” carried weight for the same reason the centurion’s orders did — it was backed by the One who sent Him.
  </p>

  <h2>Authority Over His Own Life</h2>

  <p>
    “I have power to lay it down, and I have power to take it again” <em>John 10:18</em>. The word is exousia, and the verse ends by naming its source: “This commandment have I received of my Father.” Pilate used the very same construction — “I have power to crucify thee, and have power to release thee” — and was told his exousia came from above <em>John 19:10-11</em>. The same phrase, in the same Gospel, means the same thing.
  </p>

  <h2>The Same Authority Given to Believers</h2>

  <p>
    The strongest proof that exousia is not deity is that Scripture gives it to believers in the same words:
  </p>

  <p>
    • “To them gave he power [exousia] to become the sons of God” <em>John 1:12</em>.<br />
    • He “gave them power [exousia] against unclean spirits, to cast them out” <em>Matthew 10:1</em>.<br />
    • “power [exousia] to tread on serpents and scorpions” <em>Luke 10:19</em>.<br />
    • “To him will I give power [exousia] over the nations” <em>Revelation 2:26</em>.<br />
    • “That they may have right [exousia] to the tree of life” <em>Revelation 22:14</em>.<br />
    • And over the believer, “the second death hath no power [exousia]” <em>Revelation 20:6</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Son received exousia to forgive, to judge, to command, and to take His life again. The believer receives exousia to become a son, to cast out spirits, to judge, to rule, and to eat of the tree of life. The word is the same, the Giver is the same, and the meaning is the same. If received authority proves the Son is God, it proves every believer is God. Scripture draws neither conclusion. Received authority marks the one who holds it as one who was given it.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is the point the Trinity reading cannot survive. The proof texts set out to show that Yahushua holds powers no man could hold. The word Scripture uses for those powers is the same word it uses for powers given to ordinary believers. What sets the Son apart is not a different kind of power. He received it first, in full measure, and used it perfectly — “the firstborn among many brethren” <em>Romans 8:29</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>Authority Given Back</h2>

  <p>
    Authority that is given can be handed back, and Scripture says the Son’s will be. At the end, the Son will have “delivered up the kingdom to God, even the Father,” after putting down “all rule and all authority [exousia]” <em>1 Corinthians 15:24</em>. Paul adds that when all things are put under the Son, “he is excepted, which did put all things under him,” and “then shall the Son also himself be subject unto him … that God may be all in all” <em>1 Corinthians 15:27-28</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Father is the One excepted — the only One who is never under anyone. The Son receives the kingdom, rules it, and returns it, and is subject to the Father forever after. That is the full life of received authority, from the giving to the handing back.
  </p>

  <h2>What Received Authority Proves</h2>

  <p>
    Every verse that shows Yahushua holding exousia proves two things. It proves He truly holds it — He forgives, commands, judges, and rose again, and His authority is real. And it proves He received it from Another. The one who gives authority is greater than the one who receives it, and the Son said so plainly: “my Father is greater than I” <em>John 14:28</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Yahuah the Father is the source of all exousia, holding it from no one. The Son holds it from the Father. The believer holds it from the Father through the Son. Revelation keeps that order to the end: the risen Son gives the overcomer power over the nations, “even as I received of my Father” <em>Revelation 2:27</em>.
  </p>

</FoundationLayout>
